hmmt-feb-2024-guts-18.body:
$\gcd(a+b,ab+1)=\gcd(a+b,1-b^2)$ (replace $a$ by $-b$), so $(a,b)$ is spicy iff $\gcd(a+b,b-1)=\gcd(a+b,b+1)=1$, i.e. $\gcd(a+1,b-1)=1$ and $\gcd(a-1,b+1)=1$.

So we need $\gcd(n-1,100)=\gcd(n+1,98)=\gcd(n-1,102)=\gcd(n+1,100)=1$:
- mod $2$: $n$ even ($1$ of $2$ residues);
- mod $3$: $n\not\equiv1$ ($2$ of $3$);
- mod $5$: $n\not\equiv\pm1$ ($3$ of $5$);
- mod $7$: $n\not\equiv-1$ ($6$ of $7$);
- mod $17$: $n\not\equiv1$ ($16$ of $17$).

Since $2024!$ is a multiple of all these moduli, residues are uniform and independent:
$$\frac12\cdot\frac23\cdot\frac35\cdot\frac67\cdot\frac{16}{17}=\frac{96}{595}.$$

**Answer: $\frac{96}{595}$**
